====================================================================================================
  base Q                  r  meas(base)   peel factor meas(base+{w})/meas(base) for w=...
  (1, 2)                  2     0.78571   w=50:0.8545  w=100:0.8582  w=200:0.8582  w=500:0.8567  w=1000:0.8571   (-> 6/7=0.8571)
  (2, 3)                  2     0.76190   w=50:0.8500  w=100:0.8569  w=200:0.8594  w=500:0.8568  w=1000:0.8571   (-> 6/7=0.8571)
  (1, 2, 3)               3     0.69048   w=50:0.8510  w=100:0.8586  w=200:0.8593  w=500:0.8568  w=1000:0.8572   (-> 6/7=0.8571)
  (3, 5, 7)               3     0.62041   w=50:0.8487  w=100:0.8588  w=200:0.8538  w=500:0.8579  w=1000:0.8574   (-> 6/7=0.8571)
  (1, 2, 3, 4)            4     0.61905   w=50:0.8523  w=100:0.8562  w=200:0.8592  w=500:0.8560  w=1000:0.8569   (-> 6/7=0.8571)
  (2, 3, 5, 7, 11)        5     0.40792   w=50:0.8310  w=100:0.8629  w=200:0.8590  w=500:0.8564  w=1000:0.8570   (-> 6/7=0.8571)
  CONCLUSION: peel factor -> 6/7 from BELOW as w grows; wide Q has LARGER measure
  => inf meas(Q-lonely) is on bounded Q (the dense/small minimizers).

====================================================================================================
